    "##### Social Circles Missingness"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "9254a505",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Missingness check across social circles subset\n",
    "\n",
    "cols_sc = cols_by_theme['social_circle']\n",
    "miss = df[cols_sc].isnull().sum()\n",
    "\n",
    "# split each column name into (base, suffix)\n",
    "tbl = miss.rename_axis('col').reset_index(name='n_missing')\n",
    "tbl['suffix'] = tbl['col'].str.rsplit('_', n=1).str[1]\n",
    "tbl['base']   = tbl['col'].str.rsplit('_', n=1).str[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "pivot = tbl.pivot(index='base', columns='suffix', values='n_missing')\n",
    "print(pivot)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "a6e5e476",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Pivot Shows equivalent missingness counts across each of these columns - check correlation to confirm same values missing in each column\n",
    "corr = df[cols_sc].isnull().corr()\n",
    "\n",
    "off_diag = corr.where(~np.eye(len(corr), dtype=bool))\n",
    "print(off_diag.min().min())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Visualisation to confirm\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(12, 10))\n",
    "sns.heatmap(corr,\n",
    "            cmap='RdYlGn',\n",
    "            vmin=0, vmax=1,\n",
    "            square=True,\n",
    "            linewidths=0.5,\n",
    "            annot=True,          # write the value in each square\n",
    "            fmt='.2f',           # 2 decimal places\n",
    "            annot_kws={'size': 12},\n",
    "            cbar_kws={'label': 'Nullity correlation'})\n",
    "plt.title('Missingness correlation across social_circle columns')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.yticks(rotation=0, ha='right')\n",
    "plt.xticks(rotation=45, ha='right')\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "aae139bf",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# quick crosstab check for these thematic subsets\n",
    "\n",
    "bureau_missing = df[cols_by_theme['bureau_enquiries'][0]].isnull()\n",
    "social_missing = df[cols_by_theme['social_circle'][0]].isnull()\n",
    "\n",
    "pd.crosstab(bureau_missing, social_missing)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1d159d2d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observation/Decision:** Both subsets show block-wise missingness, and the crosstab confirms they are independent of each other (minimal row overlap) - so they have separate origins. Mechanism (informative vs not) to be tested against TARGET in Section 4."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4c783c18",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 2.4 Cardinality Assessment"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d5ab0567",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Cardinality Assessment of Cateogrical Ordinal Columns. The columns 'HOUR_APPR_PROCESS_START', 'REGION_RATING_CLIENT', 'REGION_RATING_CLIENT_W_CITY' are beign dropped from this assessment as they are defined by internal ratings and the hours of the day. There are no benefits to assessing these columns in this context as no actions will be taken to; merge sparse columns, establish supervised grouping or domain grouping."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "51cc6e22",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Prediction**:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d109e6f5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(cols_by_type['categorical'])\n",
    "    \n",
    "cols = cols_by_type['categorical'] + cols_by_type['ordinal']\n",
    "\n",
    "len(cols)\n",
    "\n",
    "cardinality = df[cols].nunique().sort_values(ascending=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "cardinality = cardinality.drop(['HOUR_APPR_PROCESS_START', 'REGION_RATING_CLIENT', 'REGION_RATING_CLIENT_W_CITY'])\n",
    "\n",
    "labels = cardinality.index.str.replace('_', ' ').str.title()\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(10,6))\n",
    "\n",
    "bars = ax.barh(labels, cardinality.values,\n",
    "        color=\"tab:blue\",\n",
    "        alpha=0.7)\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel('Count of Uniqe Value in Column', labelpad=20)\n",
    "ax.set_ylabel('Column Name', labelpad=20)\n",
    "ax.set_title('Categorical Data Type Cardinality by Column')\n",
    "\n",
    "ax.bar_label(bars,\n",
    "             padding=10,\n",
    "             bbox=dict(\n",
    "                 boxstyle='round',\n",
    "                 facecolor='white',\n",
    "                 edgecolor='grey',\n",
    "                 alpha = 0.8,\n",
    "                 linewidth=0.8\n",
    "             ))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "19d8818d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observation/Decision**: The two highest cardinality columns 'OCCUPATION_TYPE' and 'ORGANIZATION_TYPE' exceed more than 10 unique entries and must have a specified treatment plan for IV/WOE binning and the following ranking of feature importance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "7d3662b5",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Assess columns with < 5% of the data for merging into an other column\n",
    "\n",
    "bins = df['OCCUPATION_TYPE'].value_counts(normalize=True, ascending=True, dropna=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "print((bins.values < 0.01).sum())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "82d83a29",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Assess columns with < 5% of the data for merging into an other column\n",
    "\n",
    "bins = df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'].value_counts(normalize=True, ascending=True, dropna=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "print((bins.values < 0.01).sum())\n",
    "\n",
    "# 41 columns that contain < 1% of the data - will look at default rate per column instead\n",
    "\n",
    "defaulters = df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'][df['TARGET'] == 1]\n",
    "\n",
    "organization_type_counts = df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'].value_counts(dropna=False)\n",
    "\n",
    "defaulters = defaulters.value_counts(dropna=False) / organization_type_counts\n",
    "\n",
    "defaulters.sort_values(ascending=False).map('{:.2%}'.format)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Sanity Check\n",
    "\n",
    "# len(df[df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'] == 'Transport: type 3'] )\n",
    "\n",
    "# len(df[(df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'] == 'Transport: type 3') & (df['TARGET'] == 1)]  )\n",
    "\n",
    "# 187 / 1187"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f1196e76",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observation:** Both are high-cardinality categoricals whose sparse levels would produce unstable WOE. ORGANIZATION_TYPE (~58 levels) will be grouped by similar default rate (see band mapping below); OCCUPATION_TYPE (~18 levels) will have levels below 1% pooled, with NaN kept as its own category."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b2f98cc6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Band Mapping for 'ORGANIZATION TYPE'\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "73091b7d",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Written by AI \n",
    "\n",
    "rate = df.groupby('ORGANIZATION_TYPE')['TARGET'].mean()\n",
    "counts = df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'].value_counts()\n",
    "\n",
    "band = pd.cut(rate, bins=[0, 0.06, 0.08, 0.10, 1.0],\n",
    "              labels=['<6%', '6-8%', '8-10%', '>10%'])\n",
    "\n",
    "# keep a high-rate level on its own only if it's big enough\n",
    "band = band.astype('object')\n",
    "band[(rate > 0.10) & (counts >= 500)] = rate.index[(rate > 0.10) & (counts >= 500)]\n",
    "\n",
    "df['ORG_BAND'] = df['ORGANIZATION_TYPE'].map(band.to_dict())\n",
    "\n",
    "df['ORG_BAND'].value_counts()\n",
    "df['ORG_BAND'].nunique()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c487e40c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Observation:** We now have only bins for computing IV/WOE. Low default count over 10% default have been grouped into the  >10% bin."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "da984dff",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Feature Analysis"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c127ad29cf28",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Purpose.** Work through each feature family end to end — characterise its distribution (**Univariate**), then test how it moves with default (**Bivariate**) — so the binning decisions in Section 4 (WOE/IV) are informed rather than arbitrary. Surface skew, outliers, sparse categories and missingness, and close every family with a Decision that states the intended transform/binning.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Approach.** Work thematically using the `cols_by_theme` scaffolding from Section 2, highest-value families first. Each family runs two beats — Univariate then Bivariate — before a single committed Decision. Keep the **Predict → Task → Decision** rhythm; each Decision should point forward to Section 4.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Planned order:**\n",
    "\n",
    "| # | Family | Columns | What to look for → decision |\n",
    "|---|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 3.1 | External scores | `EXT_SOURCE_1/2/3` | Strongest expected drivers. Shape + missingness (EXT_SOURCE_1 ~56% missing) → quantile bins, missing as its own bin? |\n",
    "| 3.2 | Loan financials | `AMT_INCOME_TOTAL`, `AMT_CREDIT`, `AMT_ANNUITY`, `AMT_GOODS_PRICE` | Heavy right-skew + outliers → log-transform vs quantile bins |\n",
    "| 3.3 | Age & employment | `DAYS_BIRTH`, `DAYS_EMPLOYED` | Convert to positive years (`/-365`); DAYS_EMPLOYED carries the sentinel NaN → bin + missing flag |\n",
    "| 3.4 | Key categoricals | education, family status, income type, contract type | Frequency bars + default rate per level; note sparse levels (links back to 2.4 cardinality) |\n",
    "| 3.5 | Other numeric families | `client_timeline`, `social_circle`, `bureau_enquiries` | Lighter touch; confirm shape, flag anything odd |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Scope note:** distributions, target relationships and binning intent — the cross-feature IV ranking and the WOE/IV encoding itself are quantified in Section 4.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "453550b5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
